EXPERIMENT 8: S3 + SNS + SQS + Lambda
Aim
To integrate AWS services for event-driven processing.
________________________________________
🧠 Architecture (understand once)
S3 (upload file)
   ↓
SNS (notification)
   ↓
SQS (queue)
   ↓
Lambda (process event)
________________________________________
🔹 STEP 1: Create S3 Bucket
1.	Go to AWS → S3 
2.	Click Create bucket 
Fill:
•	Bucket name: exp8-bucket-12345 (must be unique) 
•	Region: choose same region everywhere 
👉 Click Create bucket
________________________________________
🔹 STEP 2: Create SNS Topic
1.	Go to AWS → SNS 
2.	Click Topics → Create topic 
Select:
•	Type: Standard 
Fill:
•	Name: MyTopic 
👉 Click Create topic
________________________________________
🔹 STEP 3: Create SQS Queue
1.	Go to AWS → SQS 
2.	Click Create queue 
Select:
•	Type: Standard 
Fill:
•	Name: MyQueue 
👉 Click Create queue
________________________________________
🔹 STEP 4: Subscribe SQS to SNS
1.	Go to SNS → Topics → MyTopic 
2.	Click Create subscription 
Fill:
•	Protocol: Amazon SQS 
•	Endpoint: select MyQueue 
👉 Click Create subscription
________________________________________
🔥 STEP 5: Allow SNS to send to SQS (VERY IMPORTANT)
1.	Go to SQS → MyQueue 
2.	Click Permissions tab 
3.	Click Edit policy 
👉 Add this policy (replace ARN if needed):
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"Service": "sns.amazonaws.com"},
      "Action": "sqs:SendMessage",
      "Resource": "*"
    }
  ]
}
👉 Save
________________________________________
🔹 STEP 6: Create Lambda Function
1.	Go to AWS → Lambda 
2.	Click Create function 
Select:
•	Author from scratch 
Fill:
•	Function name: MyLambda 
•	Runtime: Python 3.x 
👉 Click Create function
________________________________________
Replace code:
def lambda_handler(event, context):
    print("Event received:", event)
    return {
        'statusCode': 200,
        'body': 'Success'
    }
👉 Click Deploy
________________________________________
🔹 STEP 7: Connect SQS → Lambda
1.	Go to Lambda → MyLambda 
2.	Click Add trigger 
Select:
•	Source: SQS 
•	Queue: MyQueue 
👉 Click Add
________________________________________
🔹 STEP 8: Configure S3 Event → SNS
1.	Go to S3 → Your bucket 
2.	Click Properties tab 
Scroll → Event notifications
👉 Click Create event notification
________________________________________
Fill:
•	Name: S3Event 
•	Event types: ✔ PUT (Object Created) 
•	Destination:
👉 SNS topic
→ select MyTopic 
👉 Click Save changes
________________________________________
🔹 STEP 9: TEST
1.	Go to S3 bucket 
2.	Click Upload 
3.	Upload any file 
________________________________________
🔥 What happens internally
•	File uploaded → S3 event triggered 
•	SNS receives event 
•	SNS → sends message to SQS 
•	SQS → triggers Lambda 
•	Lambda prints event 
________________________________________
🔹 STEP 10: Verify Output
1.	Go to Lambda → MyLambda 
2.	Click Monitor 
3.	Click View logs in CloudWatch 
________________________________________
You should see:
Event received: {...}
________________________________________
✅ Result
S3, SNS, SQS, and Lambda services were successfully integrated for event-driven processing.
________________________________________
EXPERIMENT 9: ELB + AUTO SCALING (AS PER PDF STYLE)
Aim
To implement Elastic Load Balancer with Auto Scaling using AWS EC2 instances.
________________________________________
Procedure
Step 1: Launch EC2 Instance (Template Base)
1.	Login to AWS 
2.	Go to Services → EC2 → Launch Instance 
3.	Configure: 
o	Name: WebServer 
o	AMI: Amazon Linux / Ubuntu 
o	Instance Type: t2.micro 
o	Key pair: existing 
4.	Security Group: 
o	Allow HTTP (port 80) 
o	Allow SSH (port 22) 
5.	Click Launch Instance 
________________________________________
Step 2: Install Web Server
1.	Connect to EC2 using SSH 
2.	Run: 
sudo apt update
sudo apt install apache2 -y
(for Amazon Linux use yum install httpd -y)
3.	Start service: 
sudo systemctl start apache2
________________________________________
Step 3: Create AMI
1.	Go to EC2 → Instances 
2.	Select your instance 
3.	Click: 
o	Actions → Image → Create Image 
4.	Name: MyAMI 
5.	Click Create 
________________________________________
Step 4: Create Launch Template
1.	Go to EC2 → Launch Templates 
2.	Click Create launch template 
3.	Configure: 
o	Name: MyTemplate 
o	AMI: select created AMI 
o	Instance type: t2.micro 
o	Security group: allow HTTP 
4.	Click Create 
________________________________________
Step 5: Create Target Group
1.	Go to EC2 → Target Groups 
2.	Click Create target group 
3.	Select: 
o	Type: Instances 
o	Protocol: HTTP 
4.	Click Create 
________________________________________
Step 6: Create Load Balancer
1.	Go to EC2 → Load Balancers 
2.	Click Create Load Balancer 
3.	Choose: 
o	Application Load Balancer 
4.	Configure: 
o	Scheme: Internet-facing 
o	Listener: HTTP (port 80) 
o	Select target group 
5.	Click Create 
________________________________________
Step 7: Create Auto Scaling Group
1.	Go to EC2 → Auto Scaling Groups 
2.	Click Create Auto Scaling Group 
3.	Select: 
o	Launch template (MyTemplate) 
4.	Configure: 
o	Desired capacity: 2 
o	Min: 1 
o	Max: 3 
5.	Attach to: 
o	Load balancer 
6.	Click Create 
________________________________________
Step 8: Verify
•	Open Load Balancer DNS 
•	Website should load 
•	Multiple instances handle traffic 
________________________________________
Result
Elastic Load Balancer and Auto Scaling were successfully implemented.










EXPERIMENT 10: ELASTIC BEANSTALK
Aim
To deploy an application using AWS Elastic Beanstalk.
________________________________________
What is Elastic Beanstalk (1 line)
👉 It automatically deploys and manages web applications (no EC2 setup manually)
________________________________________
Procedure (FOLLOW EXACTLY)
________________________________________
STEP 1: Go to Beanstalk
👉 AWS → Services → Elastic Beanstalk
Click:
👉 Create Application
________________________________________
STEP 2: Application Details
Fill:
•	Application name: 
MyApp
________________________________________
STEP 3: Environment
Select:
•	Environment tier: Web server environment 
________________________________________
STEP 4: Platform
Select:
•	Platform: Python 
•	Platform version: default 
________________________________________
STEP 5: Application Code
Select:
👉 Sample application
(IMPORTANT: don’t upload anything)
________________________________________
STEP 6: Presets
Select:
👉 Single instance (free tier)
________________________________________
STEP 7: Service Role (IMPORTANT)
If asked:
👉 Select existing role like:
•	LabRole 
•	aws-elasticbeanstalk-service-role 
________________________________________
STEP 8: Create
👉 Click Create application
________________________________________
⏳ Wait (2–5 minutes)
Beanstalk will:
•	Create EC2 
•	Configure environment 
•	Deploy app 
________________________________________
STEP 9: Open Application
After creation:
👉 You will see Domain URL
Click it:
http://xxxxx.elasticbeanstalk.com







